        # well-known arity for common Linux x86_64 syscalls.
        #
        # v5.8.7 (phylax #3 + sakshi): gate the check to x86_64 only.
        # The _SC_ARITY table maps x86_64 syscall numbers to expected
        # user-arg counts. On aarch64 the SAME numerical values mean
        # different syscalls with different arities (e.g. aarch64
        # SYS_NEWFSTATAT = 79 = x86's getcwd; aarch64 SYS_FSTAT = 80 =
        # x86's chdir; aarch64 SYS_OPENAT = 56 = x86's clone). Stdlib's
        # at-family wrappers in `lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr` then
        # call `syscall(SYS_NEWFSTATAT, AT_FDCWD, path, buf, 0)` (4
        # user args) and the parser flagged it as mismatching getcwd's
        # 2-arg expectation — false positive.
        #
        # Pre-v5.8.7 phylax + sakshi saw 9-11 spurious warnings on a
        # 4-line stdlib-only `cyrius build --aarch64` invocation
        # (sakshi `2026-04-30-cyrius-lang-blockers.md`, phylax
        # `2026-04-30-cyrius-stdlib-issues.md` §3). Same fix shape as
        # v5.7.8 SYS_SETSID 1→0 in spirit: pin the check to the arch
        # the table actually describes. Hand-written aarch64 syscalls
        # with wrong arity now get the runtime error rather than a
        # compile warning — acceptable tradeoff; the warning was
        # best-effort, not load-bearing.
